    "readable_text": "Item 1 \u2014 Tutorial: Introduction\n\nContent:\nEvery fraction represents a division - the division of the numerator by the denominator. For example:\n[MATH: \\dfrac{\\color{blue}1}{\\color{red}3} = {\\color{blue}1} \\div {\\color{red}3}]\n\nContent:\nLikewise, we can represent every division problem as a fraction, where the dividend is the numerator, and the divisor is the denominator. For example:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: {\\color{blue}3} \\div {\\color{red}2} = \\dfrac{\\color{blue}3}{\\color{red}2}]\n\n---\n\nItem 2 \u2014 Example: Interpreting a Division Problem as a Fraction\n\nExample Prompt:\nExpress [MATH: 59\\div 4] as a fraction.\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nA division problem may be interpreted as a fraction, where the dividend is the numerator, and the divisor is the denominator.\n\nTherefore, we can represent the given division problem as\n\n[MATH: 59\\div 4=\\dfrac{59}{4}.]\n\n---\n\nItem 3 \u2014 Question 1\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: 37 \u00f7 3 =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (37)/(3)]\nb. [MATH: (1)/(37)]\nc. [MATH: (3)/(37)]\nd. [MATH: 37]\ne. [MATH: (1)/(3)]\n\n---\n\nItem 4 \u2014 Question 2\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: 40 \u00f7 13 =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 3]\nb. [MATH: (1)/(13)]\nc. [MATH: (40)/(13)]\nd. [MATH: (13)/(40)]\ne. [MATH: (1)/(40)]\n\n---\n\nItem 5 \u2014 Example: Interpreting a Division Problem as a Fraction When Simplification is Needed\n\nExample Prompt:\nWhat is [MATH: 20\\div 8] as an improper fraction?\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nFirst, we can represent the given division problem as a fraction:\n\n[MATH: 20\\div 8 = \\dfrac{20}{8}]\n\nThen, we can simplify the fraction by dividing both the numerator and denominator by [MATH: 4\\mathbin{:}]\n\n[MATH: \\dfrac{20\\div 4}{8\\div 4} = \\dfrac{5}{2}]\n\n---\n\nItem 6 \u2014 Question 3\n\nPrompt:\nWhat is [MATH: 22 \u00f7 8] as an improper fraction?\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (13)/(2)]\nb. [MATH: (11)/(4)]\nc. [MATH: (11)/(2)]\nd. [MATH: (11)/(3)]\ne. [MATH: (13)/(4)]\n\n---\n\nItem 7 \u2014 Question 4\n\nPrompt:\nWhat is [MATH: 49 \u00f7 14] as an improper fraction?\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (9)/(2)]\nb. [MATH: (7)/(2)]\nc. [MATH: (13)/(3)]\nd. [MATH: (7)/(3)]\ne. [MATH: (13)/(7)]\n\n---\n\nItem 8 \u2014 Example: Interpreting a Fraction as a Division\n\nExample Prompt:\nExpress [MATH: \\dfrac{8}{19}] as an equivalent division problem:\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nA fraction may be interpreted as the division of the numerator by the denominator.\n\nTherefore, we can represent the given fraction as\n\n[MATH: \\dfrac{8}{19}=8\\div 19.]\n\n---\n\nItem 9 \u2014 Question 5\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: (2)/(7) =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 2 + 7]\nb. [MATH: 7 \u00f7 2]\nc. [MATH: 2 \u00f7 7]\nd. [MATH: 2 \u00d7 7]\ne. [MATH: 2 \u2212 7]\n\n---\n\nItem 10 \u2014 Question 6\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: (43)/(7) =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 1 \u00f7 (43 + 7)]\nb. [MATH: 43 + 1 \u00f7 7]\nc. [MATH: (1 + 43) \u00f7 7]\nd. [MATH: 43 \u00f7 7]\ne. [MATH: 7 \u00f7 43]"
